---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePages } from '../data/site-pages';
import themes from '../data/themes.json';
---
<Layout {...sitePages.features}>
  <section class="wrap guide-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Features / Eitr Linux</p>
    <h1>One desktop.<br /><em>Many ways to use it.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">Eitr Linux brings an Arch-based system, a native Hyprland desktop and everyday tools into one distribution. Here is what is available, what is optional, and what still needs testing.</p>
    <nav class="info-links" aria-label="Feature sections">
      <a class="text-link" href="#desktop">Desktop</a><a class="text-link" href="#development">Development &amp; AI</a><a class="text-link" href="#accounts">Accounts</a><a class="text-link" href="#organizations">Organizations</a><a class="text-link" href="#recovery">Recovery</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <div class="wrap info-body">
    <section class="info-section" id="desktop" aria-labelledby="features-desktop">
      <p class="eyebrow">Desktop &amp; appearance</p>
      <h2 id="features-desktop">Keyboard-first.<br /><em>Graphical when you need it.</em></h2>
      <p>The custom Quickshell desktop includes native notifications, a workspace Overview with live previews, and panels for audio, WiFi, Bluetooth, displays, calendar, weather and hardware. The Control Center opens with Super + comma. Walker searches applications, menus, settings and actions.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Place monitors beside one another, choose a main display, and show the bar on one or all screens.</li>
        <li>Adjust per-application audio and microphone controls in one panel.</li>
        <li>Choose from {themes.length} themes, with matching application colors, lockscreens, 4K wallpapers and ultrawide artwork.</li>
        <li>Keep your preferred bar position, corners, cursor and fonts when switching themes.</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="text-link" href="/#tour">See the real desktop →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="development" aria-labelledby="features-development">
      <p class="eyebrow">Development &amp; AI</p>
      <h2 id="features-development">Tools for the work.<br /><em>Assistance by choice.</em></h2>
      <p>Kitty, Zsh, tmux, Neovim with LazyVim, Docker, Lazygit and Lazydocker form the development workspace. Menus offer additional editors, language environments, database clients, API tools and monitoring dashboards. Plugins add your own tools from a Git repository: you see the author, the exact commit and a trust warning before anything runs, and their actions appear under Tools &gt; Plugins.</p>
      <p>Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot CLI, Grok and Gemini CLI are available through first-use installation. Antigravity is also available as a standalone assistant. Choose a default assistant, open it with Super + Alt + A, and see supported assistants’ usage limits in the bar.</p>
      <p>The optional “Let assistants work together” feature connects supported clients through a local bridge. Another signed-in assistant can give a read-only second opinion from the same working folder. Hand-offs retain the caller’s tool-approval boundary; helpers cannot delegate onward. Real-account verification remains a separate step from mock and unit tests; Gemini has answered read-only and through the bridge with a free API key; Antigravity is not a collaboration helper.</p>
      <p>Crash notifications can offer assistant help or a problem report with crash details. These are opt-in workflows; Eitr does not silently send every crash to an AI service. Provider accounts, subscriptions and network access are separate from the distribution.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/development/">Development workflows, defaults &amp; optional tools →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="accounts" aria-labelledby="features-accounts">
      <p class="eyebrow">Cloud &amp; Git</p>
      <h2 id="features-accounts">Know the account.<br /><em>Keep the context visible.</em></h2>
      <p>Git Accounts manages folder-specific identities and SSH keys. Cloud menus make provider accounts and project, subscription, region or scope selections explicit, with navigation back through account, service and resource menus.</p>
      <p>Provider menus cover AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Cloudflare, DigitalOcean, Hetzner Cloud, IBM Cloud, Netlify, Oracle Cloud and Vercel. The newer provider workflows have isolated account workspaces and mock-tested resource views; live installation, sign-in and remote context checks remain open where documented. Menu availability is not a claim that every vendor workflow has passed real-account testing.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="organizations" aria-labelledby="features-organizations">
      <p class="eyebrow">Organization profiles</p>
      <h2 id="features-organizations">Set it up once.<br /><em>Install it on every desk.</em></h2>
      <p>An organization profile is a JSON file on the installer’s answers stick. It removes reviewed optional apps, adds packages, Flathub apps and checksummed package files, sets defaults such as the theme, wallpaper, browser and an extra signed repository, and applies policies: leave out groups such as gaming or media, and hide and refuse remote access, assistant collaboration and update-channel changes. Review lists every change before the disk is touched; <code>eitr profile check</code> and <code>eitr profile apply</code> do the same on an installed machine.</p>
      <p>Policies govern Eitr’s own tools; they are not a lockdown. A user with root can edit or override them, so real lockdown also means not granting root. Installs with and without a network, a refused profile and applying a profile to an installed machine have passed in a virtual machine. An update that really upgrades packages, the hidden controls on screen and Review in each language are still to be checked.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/eitr-org/eitr/blob/main/distro/ORGANIZATION-PROFILES.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the profile format and limits ↗</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="features-everyday">
      <p class="eyebrow">Everyday tools &amp; gaming</p>
      <h2 id="features-everyday">Capture an idea.<br /><em>Make room to play.</em></h2>
      <p>Take screenshots, record with OBS, read text or QR codes from the screen, pick a color, set reminders and share nearby through LocalSend. Hold Super + D for dictation transcribed on your own computer. Hold Super + Shift + D to tell your default assistant about a reminder: it understands dates, alerts an hour before appointments, asks in a terminal when something is unclear, and the bar’s calendar marks those days. Reminders can repeat daily, on chosen weekdays, weekly, monthly or yearly, until a date or for a number of times, spoken or typed as “every Monday 09:30”, “weekdays 07:00” or “every month on the 15th”; a monthly 31st falls back to the month’s last day. After downtime, a missed reminder shows once, and cancelling asks whether to skip one occurrence or end the series. Your voice is transcribed locally; only the text goes to the assistant’s provider. Tools &gt; Music Visualizer opens cava in the theme’s colors, and the bar’s equalizer follows the music that is playing. The offline Handbook opens with Super + F1.</p>
      <p>Optional Steam, RetroArch and GeForce NOW installations, alongside included GameMode and MangoHud, provide local, classic and cloud gaming choices. Game Mode can disable animations, blur and notification popups. Individual games, controllers and cloud services have their own compatibility and account requirements.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="recovery" aria-labelledby="features-recovery">
      <p class="eyebrow">Maintenance &amp; recovery</p>
      <h2 id="features-recovery">A way forward.<br /><em>A way back.</em></h2>
      <p>System Update checks Arch news and available changes, creates a Btrfs snapshot when an upgrade is needed, and updates from Arch and Eitr’s signed repository. From a terminal, <code>eitr update pacman</code>, <code>eitr update aur</code> and <code>eitr update full</code> update without automatic snapshots. While it works, each step shows a turning rune and Bifröst-colored text, and pacman shows color and its Pac-Man progress bar.</p>
      <p>Recovery includes system snapshots, a boot guard, optional home-folder snapshots and factory-reset procedures. The Handbook describes supported layouts, destructive confirmations and limitations. Snapshots are not a substitute for an independent backup. Both factory resets and the boot guard’s rollback have passed on an encrypted disk in a virtual machine.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/guide/recovery/">Read the recovery Handbook →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="features-remote">
      <p class="eyebrow">Implemented / Awaiting real-device testing</p>
      <h2 id="features-remote">Remote access.<br /><em>Testing comes first.</em></h2>
      <p>Moonlight, Sunshine sharing and Tailscale away access have setup workflows and native status panels. Sharing remains inactive until explicitly configured. Real-device checks for video, input, audio, locking and access over Tailscale are still pending; Eitr does not yet describe remote access as verified for everyday use.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/eitr-org/eitr/blob/main/distro/REMOTE-ACCESS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the setup and security boundaries ↗</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
